const pythonFoundationIIGuide = "<h2>Suggested study route</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Tutorial</th><th>Focus</th><th>Suggested time</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A</td><td>2.1</td><td>Booleans, comparisons and decisions</td><td>75 minutes</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>2.2</td><td>Logical operators and nested decisions</td><td>60 minutes</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>2.3</td><td>Input validation and exceptions</td><td>75 minutes</td></tr><tr><td>D</td><td>2.4</td><td>For loops, ranges and counters</td><td>90 minutes</td></tr><tr><td>E</td><td>2.5</td><td>While loops and loop control</td><td>60 minutes</td></tr><tr><td>F</td><td>2.6</td><td>Functions, parameters and return values</td><td>90 minutes</td></tr><tr><td>G</td><td>2.7</td><td>Imports, debugging and integrated case</td><td>120 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The sequence follows the syllabus pathway for advanced Python foundations: decisions, repetition, reusable functions, imports and systematic debugging. Work in order if these ideas are new.</p>\n<h2>How to use every worked example</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Predict:</strong> state the branch, output or error you expect.</li>\n<li><strong>Run:</strong> execute the unmodified example in Colab.</li>\n<li><strong>Inspect:</strong> compare actual and expected behaviour.</li>\n<li><strong>Explain:</strong> trace the condition, iteration or function call.</li>\n<li><strong>Modify:</strong> change one input, threshold or rule.</li>\n<li><strong>Break:</strong> reproduce the deliberate error.</li>\n<li><strong>Repair:</strong> make the smallest relevant correction.</li>\n<li><strong>Test:</strong> include normal, invalid and boundary cases.</li>\n<li><strong>Reflect:</strong> identify the assumption or human judgement that code cannot settle.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>This extends the learning cycle used in Python Foundations I: <strong>run → explain → modify → break → repair → apply</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Recurring case</h2>\n<p>The examples use the fictional Green Mobility Consultation. Individual values and short lists keep attention on Python logic before CSV files and pandas are introduced in the next course part.</p>\n<h2>Lecture and syllabus alignment</h2>\n<p>The section implements the syllabus progression from basic instructions to conditions, loops, lists and functions, with structured debugging throughout. It prepares students to explain how a small rule-based script operates, test ordinary and edge-case inputs, and discuss the limits of automated classifications.</p>";
